WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:04.580
Year after year, a ton of incredible films
are released only to end up going largely

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:09.700
unnoticed, and 2018 is no different. But don’t
worry about missing out on all the future

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:13.840
classics slipping under the radar - we’ve
got the scoop on the very best.

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:19.240
Adapted from writer Joe Kelly and artist J.
M. Ken Niimura's graphic novel with the same

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:24.860
name, I Kill Giants stars The Conjuring 2’s
Madison Wolf as the plucky Barbara Thorson,

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:29.370
an oddball outsider with her head in the clouds,
a pair of bunny ears atop her head, and a

00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:34.739
Norse war hammer in her bag. The introverted
Barbara uses her overactive imagination to

00:00:34.739 --> 00:00:39.309
create fantasy worlds she can escape to when
things get tough at home, and when she’s

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:43.800
convinced a horde of giants is headed to Earth,
she’s the only one that can vanquish them.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:51.910
“I find giants, I hunt giants, I kill giants.”

00:00:51.910 --> 00:00:57.069
Blending together a coming-of-age tale, themes
of grief and denial, magical realism, solid

00:00:57.069 --> 00:01:02.819
CGI, and captivating performances from Wolfe
and supporting actors Imogen Poots, Zoe Saldana,

00:01:02.819 --> 00:01:08.320
and Sydney Wade, I Kill Giants is a magnificent
monster movie that's monstrously moving.

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:13.720
Murder is on the menu in writer-director Cory
Finley's Thoroughbreds, a black comedy thriller

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:18.210
that centers around childhood friends Lily
and Amanda reuniting to cook up a devious

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:22.820
plan. With the help of a hired drug dealer
turned hitman, the two women plot to take

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:28.280
out Lily's tyrannical stepfather Mark. The
murder mission takes a few unexpected turns,

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:30.600
arriving at an end you won’t see coming.

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:32.400
[Gulp.]

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:38.400
Rarely do first-time creatives get their debut
as right as Finley did with Thoroughbreds.

00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:43.090
Despite the film's lack of big-budget promotion
and pre-release hype, Rotten Tomatoes says

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:47.730
the handful of viewers who saw it in theaters
were, quote, "left squirming, laughing, and

00:01:47.730 --> 00:01:49.040
gleefully entertained.”

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:54.720
In Academy Award-winning director Sebastian
Lelio’s Disobedience, forbidden love harkens

00:01:54.730 --> 00:02:00.060
back to Romeo &amp; Juliet, but religious tension
pushes it past the breaking point and headfirst

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:06.360
into dangerous territory. Based on British
author Naomi Alderman’s 2006 novel, Disobedience

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:11.630
stars Rachel Weisz as Ronit a non-practicing
Jewish woman who returns to her former Orthodox

00:02:11.630 --> 00:02:16.500
London community she was shunned from years
earlier for a forbidden romance with a female

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:22.370
schoolmate, Rachel McAdams’ Esti. Reunited
years later, the two fall back in love - only

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:26.819
this time Esti’s husband Dovid turns their
relationship into a triangle.

00:02:26.819 --> 00:02:32.510
Lelio, creates a film that swirls with friction-filled
strifes and bitter truths. Critics have flocked

00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:36.540
to the film and David Rooney of the Hollywood
Reporter has praised it, saying,

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:41.100
"The movie's soulful reflections on collective
faith and individual freedoms get under your

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:44.440
skin, continuing to resonate after the end
credits have rolled."

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:46.120
“I want to take your picture.”

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:54.800
As commonplace as the 21st century’s technology
seems, Searching is anything but ordinary.

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:59.349
Set almost entirely on computer and smartphone
screens, the John Cho-led thriller follows

00:02:59.349 --> 00:03:04.599
David Kim, the father of 16-year-old Margot
who goes missing. With the help of the San

00:03:04.599 --> 00:03:09.510
Jose police, David combs through Margot’s
computer, retracing her online footprint in

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:14.230
the hopes of finding his daughter. What follows
is a heart-stopping journey that grows darker

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:18.349
and more complex than David - and the audience
- could have ever imagined.

00:03:18.349 --> 00:03:23.750
“I didn’t know her. I didn’t know my
daughter.”

00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:28.230
Searching’s unique approach, well-sustained
suspense, and a twist you won’t see coming

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:33.400
make it one of the best movies of 2018 - and
one of the most original thrillers in a long

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:34.280
time.

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:39.280
Rafeal Casal and Hamilton’s Daveed Diggs
teamed up to write the comedy-drama you really

00:03:39.290 --> 00:03:43.791
shouldn’t have missed out on. Blindspotting
follows Diggs’ Collin, a felon nearing the

00:03:43.791 --> 00:03:49.090
end of his year-long probation, and his best
friend Miles, played by Casal, a fellow troublemaker

00:03:49.090 --> 00:03:54.520
whose influence could cost Collin the final
72 hours of his sentencing. Where Collin longs

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:59.260
for a fresh start, Miles is on the hunt for
danger in the rapidly gentrifying streets

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:04.350
of Oakland, California. Their lives splinter
when Collin witnesses a white cop kill a black

00:04:04.350 --> 00:04:08.239
man, and Miles’ thirst for violence threatens
devastating consequences.

00:04:08.239 --> 00:04:10.310
“Don’t be who you isn't.”

00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:14.940
Many moviegoers missed Blindspotting due to
its limited rollout last summer. Its later

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:19.940
wide release came on the same day as the highly
anticipated Mission: Impossible – Fallout,

00:04:19.940 --> 00:04:24.620
which stole the spotlight that weekend. That
left Blindspotting, the film Business Insider

00:04:24.620 --> 00:04:28.800
called “one of the most unique” of 2018,
just out of view.

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:33.960
Taking a fantastic novel and translating it
into an equally good film isn’t an easy

00:04:33.980 --> 00:04:39.630
task. Where many have failed before The Miseducation
of Cameron post succeeds as a coming-of-age

00:04:39.630 --> 00:04:44.449
drama that serves as a shining example of
what can happen when a silver screen adaptation

00:04:44.449 --> 00:04:46.840
does right by its source material.

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:51.960
Based on Emily M. Danforth's 2012 novel of
the same name, the film centers on Chloë

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:56.630
Grace Moretz's Cameron Post, who is outed
as a lesbian when she's caught in the backseat

00:04:56.630 --> 00:05:02.060
of a car with another girl. Soon after, Cameron's
conservative aunt orders her to undergo conversion

00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:07.190
therapy at a center called God's Promise.
There, Cameron forms friendships with a handful

00:05:07.190 --> 00:05:11.520
of other teens who, like her, put up a fight
against being "re-educated."

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:14.120
“I’m tired of feeling disgusted with myself.”

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:19.130
A limited release that didn't register on
most people's radar, The Miseducation of Cameron

00:05:19.130 --> 00:05:22.250
Post has been praised by Glenn Whipp of the
Los Angeles Times as

00:05:22.250 --> 00:05:26.880
"[...] a powerful rejoinder to anyone who'd
shame people into denying their authentic

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:27.920
selves."

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:33.160
From Academy Award-nominated director and
co-writer Debra Granik, Leave No Trace begins

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:38.151
just before the boiling point: war veteran
Will, played by Ben Foster, and his 13-year-old

00:05:38.151 --> 00:05:43.080
daughter Tom have been living in an isolated
section of a public park. But when the survivalist

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:46.480
duo make one small misstep, social services
comes calling.

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:48.360
“Do you feel safe living with your dad?”

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:49.920
“We didn’t need to be rescued.”

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:53.620
“Your dad needs to provide you shelter and
a place to live.”

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:54.620
“He did.”

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:58.930
They're taken in for extensive evaluations
and set up with everything they need to live

00:05:58.930 --> 00:06:03.639
a "normal" life: hot meals, clean clothes,
stable shelter in the suburbs, school for

00:06:03.639 --> 00:06:08.419
Tom, a job for Will. While Tom tries her hardest
to adapt to her new environment and embrace

00:06:08.419 --> 00:06:13.080
her surroundings, her father grows increasingly
rigid until he decides to take Tom back into

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:14.640
the wilderness.

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:16.080
"We need to adapt."

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:20.520
“We’re wearing their clothes, we’re
in their house, we’re eating their food,

00:06:20.530 --> 00:06:23.570
we’re doing their work, we have adapted.”

00:06:23.570 --> 00:06:27.479
Leave No Trace definitely made its mark on
audiences who caught it during its limited

00:06:27.479 --> 00:06:32.930
release run in June. San Diego Reader's Scott
Marks had the most incisive point, saying,

00:06:32.930 --> 00:06:35.200
"Oscar will remember this movie."

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:39.790
Award-winning director Steven Soderbergh casts
aside the traditional trappings of filmmaking

00:06:39.790 --> 00:06:46.300
and embraces B-movie mojo with Unsane, a psychological
horror-thriller pic shot entirely on an iPhone

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:47.310
7 Plus.

00:06:47.310 --> 00:06:52.509
Claire Foy leads as Sawyer Valentini, a financial
analyst who moved away from her hometown in

00:06:52.509 --> 00:06:57.910
an attempt to evade her longtime stalker,
David. Even hundreds of miles away, Sawyer’s

00:06:57.910 --> 00:07:02.060
still experiencing the effects of David's
torments, and admits to her therapist that

00:07:02.060 --> 00:07:06.960
she's had self-destructive thoughts. Sawyer’s
confession lands her in a mental institution

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:08.259
against her will.

00:07:08.259 --> 00:07:12.580
“I just needed to talk to someone and the
counselor said that I… did she tell you

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:13.580
to do this?”

00:07:13.580 --> 00:07:17.030
“Ms. Valentini, are you refusing to cooperate?”

00:07:17.030 --> 00:07:21.720
As if being locked away in a psychiatric hospital
wasn’t jarring enough, Sawyer’s soon forced

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:26.770
to confront her darkest fear while trapped
inside those walls. The only question is:

00:07:26.770 --> 00:07:29.960
Is the fear real, or just a product of her
paranoia?

00:07:29.960 --> 00:07:31.630
“I must be insane.”

00:07:31.630 --> 00:07:36.170
Despite its experimental nature and it being
a Soderbergh joint, Unsane didn’t drum up

00:07:36.170 --> 00:07:41.140
as much buzz as you’d expect. Still, though,
it hasn't gone without critical praise. The

00:07:41.140 --> 00:07:42.849
New Yorker's Richard Brody calls Unsane

00:07:42.849 --> 00:07:47.410
"[...] one of [Soderbergh's] best movies,
[…] the very spark of his artistic passion."

00:07:47.410 --> 00:07:49.680
All the more reason to see it yourself.

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:55.400
One part pulpy crime story, one part heart-grabbing
drama, Bomb City is a brilliant little film

00:07:55.410 --> 00:07:59.990
that made a bang without the general public
even noticing. The Jameson Brooks-directed

00:07:59.990 --> 00:08:05.120
film follows Brian, a punk music-obsessed
teen from Amarillo, Texas, who sticks out

00:08:05.120 --> 00:08:09.980
like a mohawk-adorned thumb in his conservative
town. Thankfully, there are a number of other

00:08:09.980 --> 00:08:14.250
punks out there, who view Brian as a sort
of mouthpiece for the movement. But there

00:08:14.250 --> 00:08:19.340
are an equal number of jocks who regularly
clash with the punks. Tensions bubble below

00:08:19.340 --> 00:08:24.320
the surface, then explode into a violent altercation
that comes with a fatal consequence.

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:27.440
“I lost my self control.”

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:32.720
Director Brooks based Bomb City on the real-life
story of 19-year-old Brian Deneke, a punk

00:08:32.730 --> 00:08:37.760
who was killed in a furious fracas with the
Texas town's seemingly clean-cut jocks. Not

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:41.789
only does the film present the tragedy of
Brian's death in a truthful and harrowing

00:08:41.789 --> 00:08:47.730
light, it also serves as a reminder that the
American justice system isn't always moral.

00:08:47.730 --> 00:08:50.780
As Danielle White of The Austin Chronicle
aptly puts it,

00:08:50.780 --> 00:08:55.800
"The film's message, which it wields like
a war chain, is a timeless one: Don't be such

00:08:55.800 --> 00:08:58.520
a d--- to people because they look different
from you."

00:08:58.520 --> 00:09:03.760
Joaquin Phoenix is in top form in You Were
Never Really Here, writer-director Lynne Ramsay's

00:09:03.779 --> 00:09:09.490
film adaptation of Jonathan Ames' novel of
the same name. Phoenix gives a darkly fervid

00:09:09.490 --> 00:09:14.640
performance as Joe, a former law enforcement
agent and combat veteran struggling with PTSD.

00:09:15.240 --> 00:09:17.480
“McLeary said you were brutal.”

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:23.040
“I can be.”

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:28.490
Post-FBI, Joe supports himself and his ailing
mother by locating missing and trafficked

00:09:28.490 --> 00:09:33.210
young girls. On the brink of committing to
one of his many suicidal fantasies, Joe’s

00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:38.660
boss tasks him with finding 13-year-old Nina,
the daughter of a New York State Senator,

00:09:38.660 --> 00:09:43.890
who offers Joe a sizable sum to discreetly
bring Nina home. Though Joe is properly equipped

00:09:43.890 --> 00:09:48.610
for the job, what he isn't prepared for is
the triple-layer twist and the searing horrors

00:09:48.610 --> 00:09:49.670
that await him.

00:09:49.670 --> 00:09:55.080
Variety’s Guy Lodge called the film "stark,
sinewy, [and] slashed-to-the-bone." You Were

00:09:55.080 --> 00:09:59.630
Never Really Here is more than worthy of the
global adulation its heavily-promoted cinematic

00:09:59.630 --> 00:10:01.270
counterparts have received this year.

